From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Date: 	Mon, 5 Dec 1994 15:35:03 -1000
Subject: [#94-12-183] Knowledge problem

At 5:50 AM 12/5/94, Tim Starr wrote (quoting John K Clark):
>>of it. Tim Starr says that Friedman didn't handle the "knowledge
>>problem" well , could you elaborate, are you referring to the
>>"efficient market theory"? Also I've been thinking of ordering "
>>The Enterprise of Law" but it's rather expensive, is it worth it?

Great book. If you can't afford it, a good library should have a copy. It
contains lots of historical examples of people doing things without
government that are "impossible" to do without government.

>By "the knowledge problem," I refer to the difficulty of knowing
>what another's preferences are or would be.

At most, this is only a part of the knowledge problem (the problem of
subjectivity), usually considered the least important from a modern
Austrian perspective. The knowledge problem also includes the imputation
problem (how to choose means of production given preferences over consumer
goods) and the calculation problem (how to convert the value of goods into
a common unit of value). Imputation and calculation overlap, while
subjectivity is distinct. Hayek more or less defines it in "The Use of
Knowledge in Society" as the practical problem of arriving at the knowledge
of things that the usual formulation of the economic problem takes as
given. But he does not there dub it "the knowledge problem", and neither of
the two books I thought of immediately as most relevant (Lavoie's "Rivalry
and Central Planning" and Boettke's "Why Perestroika Failed") define it or
index it. It's apparently part of the Austrian oral culture, but not so
much a part of the written culture.

The subjectivity aspect of the knowledge problem seems less important to me
because the Austrian side of the calculation debate (by my understanding,
following Lavoie) rested entirely on the calculation/imputation side of the
knowledge problem. Since they were arguing with socialists who didn't care
whether the economy adjusted to individuals' preferences, the Austrians
concentrated on showing that even a dictator who thinks he knows the
"correct" relative values of all consumer goods still needs a rivalrous
process like a market to solve the calculation and imputation problems.

Dave
tburns@mason1.gmu.edu



