Exactly the same principle applies -- reputation -- and the value
of a good reputation goes up as the value of the service goes up.

Just as today we have Aunt Sophia for macaroni, Consumers Union for
dandruff shampoo, stereos and cars, and Underwriters Laboratories for
electrical gear, we have "guilds" like the American Board of Family 
Practice, etc. to rate physicians. A professional who does not
belong/subscribe to such a service can expect to lose out to those
who do.

You see, if today I want to choose a lawyer or doctor, I do what
generations before me did -- I ask my friends (zillions of them on
the Internet) for good and bad recommendations, I look for 
institutional imprimatur, and I and my family reserve the right to 
sue the ass off of anyone who screws up after claiming he wouldn't.

You do the same, I'll bet, and the government only muddies the waters.

