Date: 	Mon, 5 Dec 1994 16:20:25 -1000
Subject: [#94-12-187] The Machinery Of Friedman

At 7:33 AM 12/5/94, William John wrote:
> People are just too busy to make well-informed
>decisions about what hospital to go to for brain surgery, or what arbitrator to
>go to for a decision about a violated business contract.  In addition, when
>these kind of situations arise (a brain tumor or a legal dispute), quick action
>is usually desirable, and time and money spent informing oneself about and
>choosing a judge or a doctor could be time and money that one could ill
>afford.
>
>In other words, choosing a doctor or judge is much different from choosing the
>cheapest or best brand of macaroni and cheese at the supermarket, and I don't
>think we can expect the same principles to apply.

Does that mean people aren't "too busy to make well-informed decisions
about" groceries/macaroni? It's the process, not the people. Competition
makes it easier to make well-informed decisions, not harder. Or rather, it
makes it easier to make good decisions without being well-informed.

I used to buy the argument about the market for medical care being poorly
suited to competition (though I've never heard this "people are too busy"
angle, which I find strange). The rationale was that you want a doctor that
knows you and your medical history, who's made an investment in you. But I
don't buy it anymore. HMO's and other trends are eroding this to the extent
it was ever really a factor. I've been switching doctors a lot recently
(mostly due to my nomadic lifestyle) and I don't think I've lost as much as
I might gain under a more competitive system. (Sounds like an
entrepreneurial opportunity for someone in keeping medical records).

